ERR1 = "Do not reference a variable outside this task in a selective accept guard"
ERR2 = "Mark this variable pragma Volatile or Atomic, or use a protected object, since it is shared unprotected between tasks"


def ids():
    return ("ADA95_6.2.4",)


def name(id):
    return {
        "ADA95_6.2.4": "Published Standards/Ada 95/6.2.4 Unprotected Shared Variables",
    }[id]


def tags(id):
    return {
        "ADA95_6.2.4": [
            "Language: Ada",
            "Standard: Ada 95",
            "Concurrency",
        ],
    }.get(id, [])


def detailed_description(id):
    return {
        "ADA95_6.2.4": """
<p><b>Guideline</b></p>
<p>&#8226; Use calls on protected subprograms or entries to pass data
between tasks rather than unprotected shared variables.</p>
<p>&#8226; Do not use unprotected shared variables as a task
synchronization device.</p>
<p>&#8226; Do not reference nonlocal variables in a guard.</p>
<p>&#8226; If an unprotected shared variable is necessary, use the pragma
Volatile or Atomic.</p>

<p><b>Example</b></p>
<p>This code will either print the same line more than once, fail to
print some lines, or print garbled lines (part of one line followed by
part of another) nondeterministically. This is because there is no
synchronization or mutual exclusion between the task that reads a
command and the one that acts on it. Without knowledge about their
relative scheduling, the actual results cannot be predicted:</p>
<pre><code language="Ada">-----------------------------------------------------------------------
task body Line_Printer_Driver is
   ...
begin
   loop
       Current_Line := Line_Buffer;
       -- send to device
   end loop;
end Line_Printer_Driver;
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

task body Spool_Server is
   ...
begin
   loop
       Disk_Read (Spool_File, Line_Buffer);
   end loop;
end Spool_Server;
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
</code></pre>
<p>The following example shows a vending machine that dispenses the
amount requested into an appropriately sized container. The guards
reference the global variables Num_Requested and Item_Count, leading to
a potential problem in the wrong amount being dispensed into an
inappropriately sized container:</p>
<pre><code language="Ada">Num_Requested : Natural;
Item_Count    : Natural := 1000;
task type Request_Manager (Personal_Limit : Natural := 1) is
   entry Make_Request (Num : Natural);
   entry Get_Container;
   entry Dispense;
end Request_Manager;

task body Request_Manager is
begin
   loop
      select
         accept Make_Request (Num : Natural) do
             Num_Requested := Num;
         end Make_Request;
      or
            when Num_Requested < Item_Count =>
               accept Get_Container;
               ...
       or
            when Num_Requested < Item_Count =>
               accept Dispense do
                  if Num_Requested <= Personal_Limit then
                     Ada.Text_IO.Put_Line ("Please pick up items.");
                  else
                     Ada.Text_IO.Put_Line ("Sorry! Requesting too many items.");
                  end if;
               end Dispense;
      end select;
   end loop;
end Request_Manager;
R1 : Request_Manager (Personal_Limit => 10);
R2 : Request_Manager (Personal_Limit => 2);
</code></pre>
<p>The interleaving of the execution of R1 and R2 can lead to
Num_Requested being changed before the entry call to Dispense is
accepted. Thus, R1 might receive fewer items than requested or R2's
request might be bounced because the request manager thinks that what R2
is requesting exceeds R2's personal limit. By using the local variable,
you will dispense the correct amount. Furthermore, by using the pragma
Volatile (Ada Reference Manual 1995, &sect;C.6), you ensure that the
Item_Count is reevaluated when the guards are evaluated. Given that the
variable Item_Count is not updated in this task body, the compiler might
otherwise have optimized the code and not generated code to reevaluate
Item_Count every time it is read:</p>
<pre><code language="Ada">Item_Count : Natural := 1000;
pragma Volatile (Item_Count);
task body Request_Manager is
   Local_Num_Requested : Natural := 0;
begin
   loop
      select
         accept Make_Request (Num : Natural) do
             Local_Num_Requested := Num;
         end Make_Request;
       or
            when Local_Num_Requested <= Personal_Limit =>
               accept Get_Container;
               ...
        or
             when Local_Num_Requested < Item_Count =>
                accept Dispense do
                   ... -- output appropriate message if couldn't service request
                end Dispense;
                Item_Count := Item_Count - Local_Num_Requested;
        end select;
     end loop;
  end Request_Manager;
</code></pre>

<p><b>Rationale</b></p>
<p>There are many techniques for protecting and synchronizing data
access. You must program most of them yourself to use them. It is
difficult to write a program that shares unprotected data correctly. If
it is not done correctly, the reliability of the program suffers.</p>
<p>Ada provides protected objects that encapsulate and provide
synchronized access to protected data that is shared between tasks.
Protected objects are expected to provide better performance than the
rendezvous that usually requires introduction of an additional task to
manage the shared data. The use of unprotected shared variables is more
error-prone than the protected objects or rendezvous because the
programmer must ensure that the unprotected shared variables are
independently addressable and that the actions of reading or updating
the same unprotected shared variable are sequential (Ada Reference
Manual 1995, &sect;9.10; Rationale 1995, &sect;II.9).</p>
<p>The first example above has a race condition requiring perfect
interleaving of execution. This code can be made more reliable by
introducing a flag that is set by Spool_Server and reset by
Line_Printer_Driver. An if (condition flag) then delay ... else would be
added to each task loop in order to ensure that the interleaving is
satisfied. However, notice that this approach requires a delay and the
associated rescheduling. Presumably, this rescheduling overhead is what
is being avoided by not using the rendezvous.</p>
<p>You might need to use an object in shared memory to communicate data
between (Rationale 1995, &sect;C.5):</p>
<ul>
<li>Ada tasks</li>
<li>An Ada program and concurrent non-Ada processes</li>
<li>An Ada program and hardware devices</li>
</ul>
<p>If your environment supports the Systems Programming Annex (Ada
Reference Manual 1995, Annex C), you should indicate whether loads and
stores to the shared object must be indivisible. If you specify the
pragma Atomic, make sure that the object meets the underlying hardware
requirements for size and alignment.</p>
<p>Multiple tasks sharing the predefined random number generator and
certain input/output subprograms can lead to problems with unprotected
updates to shared state. The Ada Reference Manual (1995, &sect;A.5.2)
points out the need for tasks to synchronize their access to the random
number generators (packages Ada.Numerics.Float_Random and
Ada.Numerics.Discrete_Random). See Guideline 7.7.5 for the I/O issue.</p>

<p><b>Developer's Note</b></p>
<p>"Do not reference nonlocal variables in a guard" is automated: each
selective accept statement's guards are located the same way
<code>ADA95_6.2.2</code> locates them, and every variable (excluding constants,
which cannot change) referenced within a guard is checked against the
enclosing task, the same way <code>ADA95_6.1.1</code> checks an entry barrier
against its enclosing protected object &#8212; comparing by <code>longname()</code>
rather than entity id for the same reason documented there.</p>
<p>The other three bullets are automated together as one check: a
package-level variable (excluding constants, and excluding an object of
a protected or task type, since those are already synchronized and are
themselves the guideline's own recommended fix) is flagged if it is
referenced (read or written, ignoring its own initializer) from within
two or more different tasks, or from within a task and from anywhere
outside any task, unless it is the argument of a <code>pragma Volatile</code> or
<code>pragma Atomic</code>. Since the variable's declaration, its pragma, and its
task-body usages can each live in a different file (a package spec
versus its body being the common case), this runs as a project-level
check (rather than the usual once-per-file check) so it can walk every
Ada file exactly once and correlate across them; <code>ent.refs()</code> from a
variable's own perspective was found, empirically, to only see
references local to whichever single file originated the query, so a
per-file check has no way to see this on its own. This is a
structural proxy, not a proof of an actual race: it does not attempt to
reason about activation order, so a variable that is written once during
elaboration and only read by a single task thereafter can still be
flagged if some other, already-completed code also touched it. Whether
the guideline's own preferred fix (routing the data through a protected
object or rendezvous instead) or its fallback (marking the variable
Volatile or Atomic) is more appropriate is a design decision left to the
developer; the check only confirms that neither is currently in place.
The nonlocal-guard check above, by contrast, still only sees references
within the file being checked, so a guard referencing a variable
declared in a different file is not detected.</p>
""",
    }[id]


def test_entity(file):
    return False


def test_global():
    return True


def test_language(language):
    return language == "Ada"


_OPENER_KEYWORDS = ("if", "case", "loop", "select", "begin", "do")
_ALTERNATIVE_KEYWORDS = ("accept", "delay", "terminate")


def _next_significant(lex):
    lex = lex.next()

    while lex and lex.token() in ("Whitespace", "Newline", "Comment"):
        lex = lex.next()

    return lex


def _is_statement_start(lex):
    prev = lex.previous(True, True)

    return prev is None or prev.text() in (";", "then", "else", "loop", "begin", "do")


def _scan_alternative(lex):
    # A guard is skipped by scanning to its alternative keyword directly,
    # rather than hunting for its own "=>", since none of accept/delay/
    # terminate can appear as an identifier inside a guard's expression.
    # The guard's own span (start of its condition through the token just
    # before the alternative keyword) is recorded for the nonlocal-
    # reference check.
    guard_span = None

    if lex and lex.token() == "Keyword" and lex.text().lower() == "when":
        start = _next_significant(lex)
        lex = start
        last = start

        while lex and lex.text().lower() not in _ALTERNATIVE_KEYWORDS:
            last = lex
            lex = _next_significant(lex)

        if start:
            guard_span = (
                start.line_begin(),
                start.column_begin(),
                last.line_begin(),
                last.column_end(),
            )

    alt_kind = lex.text().lower() if lex else None

    return alt_kind, guard_span, _next_significant(lex) if lex else None


def _scan_select_guards(lex):
    # lex is positioned right after the statement's own "select" keyword.
    # See ADA95_6.2.2 for the depth-counting rationale: a single counter
    # over if/case/loop/select/begin/do, closed by any "end", is enough
    # to skip nested constructs and find this select's own top-level
    # "or"/"end select".
    _, guard_span, lex = _scan_alternative(lex)
    guard_spans = [guard_span] if guard_span else []
    depth = 0

    while lex:
        text = lex.text().lower() if lex.token() == "Keyword" else None

        if text in _OPENER_KEYWORDS:
            depth += 1
            lex = _next_significant(lex)
            continue

        if text == "end":
            if depth == 0:
                return guard_spans

            depth -= 1
            lex = _next_significant(lex)
            continue

        if depth == 0 and text == "or":
            lex = _next_significant(lex)
            _, guard_span, lex = _scan_alternative(lex)

            if guard_span:
                guard_spans.append(guard_span)

            continue

        lex = _next_significant(lex)

    return guard_spans


def _find_guard_spans(lexer, start_line, end_line):
    spans = []
    lex = lexer.lexeme(start_line, 1)

    while lex and lex.line_begin() <= end_line:
        if (
            lex.token() == "Keyword"
            and lex.text().lower() == "select"
            and _is_statement_start(lex)
        ):
            spans.extend(_scan_select_guards(_next_significant(lex)))

        lex = lex.next()

    return spans


def _task_ranges(file):
    # A task type's spec and body each have their own "end X;" line, in
    # whichever file declares that part; ent.refs("End") returns both, so
    # the result must be filtered to this file before taking the max, or
    # a spec's "end" in a different file can outrank the body's real one.
    ranges = []

    for decl_ref in file.filerefs("Declare Body", "Task"):
        ent = decl_ref.ent()
        end_refs = [r for r in ent.refs("End") if r.file().id() == file.id()]

        if not end_refs:
            continue

        ranges.append((decl_ref.line(), max(r.line() for r in end_refs), ent))

    return ranges


def _in_span(ref, span):
    start_line, start_col, end_line, end_col = span
    line, col = ref.line(), ref.column()

    if line < start_line or line > end_line:
        return False

    if line == start_line and col < start_col:
        return False

    if line == end_line and col > end_col:
        return False

    return True


def _check_guards(check, file):
    lexer = file.lexer(lookup_ents=False)

    for start, end, task_ent in _task_ranges(file):
        spans = _find_guard_spans(lexer, start, end)

        if not spans:
            continue

        for use_ref in file.filerefs("Use, Set ~Init", "Object ~Constant"):
            obj_ent = use_ref.ent()

            if not any(_in_span(use_ref, span) for span in spans):
                continue

            obj_parent = obj_ent.parent()

            if obj_parent is not None and str(obj_parent.longname()) != str(
                task_ent.longname()
            ):
                check.violation(
                    obj_ent, file, use_ref.line(), use_ref.column(), ERR1
                )


def _volatile_or_atomic_ids(lexer):
    ids = set()
    lex = lexer.first()

    while lex:
        if lex.token() == "Keyword" and lex.text().lower() == "pragma":
            name_lex = _next_significant(lex)

            if name_lex and name_lex.text().lower() in ("volatile", "atomic"):
                paren = _next_significant(name_lex)

                if paren and paren.text() == "(":
                    arg_lex = _next_significant(paren)
                    ent = arg_lex.ent() if arg_lex else None

                    if ent:
                        ids.add(ent.id())

        lex = lex.next()

    return ids


def _package_level_variables(file):
    variables = []

    for decl_ref in file.filerefs("Declare", "Object ~Constant ~Protected ~Task"):
        ent = decl_ref.ent()
        parent = ent.parent()

        if parent is not None and parent.kind().check("Package"):
            variables.append((ent, decl_ref))

    return variables


def _location_key(ref, task_ranges):
    for start, end, task_ent in task_ranges:
        if start <= ref.line() <= end:
            return task_ent.id()

    return None


def _project_wide_locations_and_protected_ids(files):
    # A variable's declaration, its task-body usage, and a pragma
    # Volatile/Atomic covering it can all live in different files (a
    # package spec versus its body being the common case), so this walks
    # every Ada file in the project once, rather than per file checked;
    # this check runs as a project-level check (test_global) specifically
    # so this scan happens exactly once, not once per file.
    locations_by_id = {}
    protected_ids = set()

    for f in files:
        task_ranges = _task_ranges(f)
        protected_ids |= _volatile_or_atomic_ids(f.lexer(lookup_ents=True))

        for use_ref in f.filerefs("Use, Set ~Init", "Object"):
            var_id = use_ref.ent().id()
            key = _location_key(use_ref, task_ranges)
            locations_by_id.setdefault(var_id, set()).add(key)

    return locations_by_id, protected_ids


def _check_shared_variables(check, files):
    locations_by_id, protected_ids = _project_wide_locations_and_protected_ids(
        files)

    for f in files:
        for ent, decl_ref in _package_level_variables(f):
            if ent.id() in protected_ids:
                continue

            locations = locations_by_id.get(ent.id(), set())

            if len(locations) >= 2:
                check.violation(ent, f, decl_ref.line(),
                                decl_ref.column(), ERR2)


def check(check):
    files = list(check.db().ents("Ada File"))

    for f in files:
        _check_guards(check, f)

    _check_shared_variables(check, files)
